>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `register_frac_type_real`

``` hol4
Refute.register_frac_type_real : unit -> unit
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Restores or refreshes Refute's `real` fraction registration on the same
Frac carrier used for `rat`.

The registration maps the nullary `real` type and its arithmetic,
comparison, and numeral constants to the corresponding normalized
`refute` fraction operations.  `real` has no literal constructor of its
own (unlike `rat$rat_cons`), so this registration has no analogue of
`rat`'s twelfth ersatz row.  It also installs the display postprocessor
that reconstructs model values as real literals: a reconstructed Frac
atom prints as the bare numerator
when the denominator is `1` or the numerator is `0`, and otherwise as
`numerator / denominator`.  A `real` model value never prints with
`rat_cons`'s `//` syntax.

A finite Frac scope never exhausts the rationals, so this encoding
never claims exhaustion over `real`: it can find a genuine `real`
counterexample witnessed by a rational value, but it can never
conclude `NoCounterexample` for a `real` goal.  Rational values are
real values and the eleven ersatz operations agree with the real
operations on rational arguments, so for a goal whose real-typed
constants all lie in this eleven-row fragment, a Frac counterexample is
a genuine `real` counterexample; the rationals being a proper subset of
the reals costs completeness only, never soundness, within that
fragment.  Other real constants -- `real_of_hreal`/`hreal_of_real`, or
`realTheory$sup`, whose completeness axiom no finite rational carrier
realises -- are not covered by these rows; a goal using them stays
sound through the ordinary choice-guard/`unknown` machinery and can
only reach `Potential`, never this fragment's stronger `Genuine`.

Certification (`certify=true`, the default) is attempted on a `real`
Frac counterexample, but not exactly as on any other: the qualifying
binding is dropped from the certification env rather than authorized,
and the atom's genuine real-literal value is instead offered to replay
separately as an untrusted candidate hint.  A linear real-arithmetic
leaf can then certify a goal computeLib alone cannot; a nonlinear
instance still comes back inconclusive (`cert = NONE`, `certainty`
unchanged), which the trust model accepts rather than blocks.  A
successful replay reports `model = NONE` and drops any `evals` row
whose value still has a free variable, since only kernel-established
values are ever shown.  `rat` counterexamples are unaffected by this.

The operation validates all fraction metadata before changing state and
commits the fraction and display registrations together.  It is
session-local, synchronized, atomic, and idempotent.  The registration is
installed when `Refute` is loaded; this entry point is useful after
session-level customization of the `real` fraction or postprocessor
rows.  It creates no HOL objects.

### Failure

Fails if the required `realax` or `refute` constants are unavailable or
if `real` has an incompatible datatype or codatatype classification.  A
failure leaves both registries unchanged.

### See also

[`Refute.register_frac_type_rat`](#Refute.register_frac_type_rat),
[`Refute.register_frac_type`](#Refute.register_frac_type),
[`Refute.register_ersatz`](#Refute.register_ersatz),
[`Refute.register_term_postprocessor`](#Refute.register_term_postprocessor),
[`Refute.model_refute`](#Refute.model_refute)

Real model display is described in \ref{Description:the-holrefute-library}.
